INFO     rag_service.services.generation_service:generation_service.py:427 {"event": "rag_query_trace", "query": "What about corticosteroids?", "retrieval_query": null, "system_prompt_version": "440b2406", "retrieved_chunks": [{"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 23, "section": "1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects", "score": null, "scores": [0.6954414, 0.6954414, 0.1482937, 0.1482937], "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 37, "section": "4.3.3 Other Restrictions", "score": null, "scores": [0.02964751, 0.02964751], "had_context": true}], "context_char_count": 4320, "response": "Source: Protocol Ulcerative Colitis.pdf\n\nThe protocol mentions corticosteroids in two key contexts:\n\n**1. Limitations as a treatment (Benefit/Risk Aspects):**\nOral, systemic corticosteroids are used for induction of remission in patients not responding to 5-ASA. However, they have frequent and occasionally serious side effects, including:\n- Hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA)-axis suppression\n- Hyperglycaemia/insulin resistance\n- Cataracts\n- Osteoporosis (notably, IBD itself is a risk factor for osteopenia/osteoporosis)\n\nFor these reasons, systemic corticosteroids are considered undesirable in maintenance treatment. Additionally, since thiopurines and corticosteroids are not effective in all patients \u2014 especially in the moderately to severely active IBD population \u2014 there is a need for novel, effective, and safe second- and even third-line treatment options like TJ301.\n[Section 1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects \u00b7 p.23]\n\n**2. Concomitant use during the trial (Other Restrictions):**\nPatients on stable-dose concomitant treatment for UC at Visit 2 must remain on a stable dose throughout the trial. However, corticosteroids are an **exception**: tapering of corticosteroids is allowed at the discretion of the Investigator.\n[Section 4.3.3 Other Restrictions \u00b7 p.37]", "sources": [{"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 23, "section": "1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects", "exactText": "Oral, systemic corticosteroids are used for induction of remission in patients not responding to 5-ASA. Corticosteroids have frequent and occasionally serious side effects, e.g. hypothalamicpituitary-adrenal (HPA)-axis suppression, hyperglycaemia/insulin resistance, cataracts, and osteoporosis (notably, IBD itself is a risk factor for osteopenia/osteoporosis, with Crohn's patients being most affected), and systemic corticosteroids are therefore undesirable in maintenance treatment.", "bboxes": [[71.99999999999972, 338.18398291015615, 552.8399999999998, 176.0949752178484]], "relevance": "high"}, {"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 23, "section": "1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects", "exactText": "Since thiopurines and corticosteroids are not effective in all patients and especially in the moderately to severely active IBD patient population, there is a need for novel, effective and safe second- and even third-line treatment options.", "bboxes": [[71.99999999999972, 338.18398291015615, 552.8399999999998, 176.0949752178484]], "relevance": "high"}, {"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 37, "section": "4.3.3 Other Restrictions", "exactText": "Patients on stable-dose concomitant treatment for UC at Visit 2 must remain on a stable dose throughout the trial except for patients on corticosteroids; tapering of corticosteroids is allowed at the discretion of the Investigator.", "bboxes": [[72.0000000000002, 323.7839829101563, 543.8400000000001, 283.1349752178486]], "relevance": "high"}], "timing": {"response_cache_hit": false, "semantic_cache_hit": false, "chunks_compressed": true, "condense_ms": 0.001166015863418579, "query_condensed": false, "embedding_ms": 635.5670001357794, "embedding_cache_hit": false, "retrieval": {"chunk_cache_hit": false, "confidence_filter_applied": true, "confidence_filter_threshold": 0.04, "confidence_filter_bm25_threshold": null, "confidence_filter_input_count": 63, "confidence_filter_