INFO     rag_service.services.generation_service:generation_service.py:427 {"event": "rag_query_trace", "query": "(2) And when is it assessed?", "retrieval_query": "When is the primary endpoint assessed?", "system_prompt_version": "440b2406", "retrieved_chunks": [{"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 68, "section": "9.6.2 Primary Endpoint", "score": null, "scores": [0.9890976, 0.91117966], "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 29, "section": "3.4.1 Trial Design", "score": null, "scores": [0.9863631, 0.9863631], "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 24, "section": "Primary Endpoints", "score": 0.98588216, "scores": null, "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 30, "section": "3.4.2 Selection of Endpoints", "score": 0.9719925, "scores": null, "had_context": true}], "context_char_count": 6358, "response": "Source: Protocol Ulcerative Colitis.pdf\n\nThe primary endpoint (clinical and endoscopic remission) is assessed at **Week 12**.\n\n1. The primary efficacy endpoint is clinical and endoscopic remission at Week 12 in the ITT population.\n[Section 9.6.2 Primary Endpoint \u00b7 p.68]\n\n2. In order to capture the full extent of the potential treatment effect, the primary endpoint will be assessed at Week 12. As shown in the recent proof-of-concept trial with etrolizumab in a similar trial population, placebo response was higher earlier during the treatment course (at Week 6 as opposed to Week 10). Thus, more substantive 'true' response and remission rates can be expected with longer treatment duration.\n[Section 3.4.1 Trial Design \u00b7 p.29]\n\n3. The prospectively defined primary efficacy endpoint will be a binary endpoint of clinical and endoscopic remission at Week 12.\n[Section 3.4.2 Selection of Endpoints \u00b7 p.30]", "sources": [{"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 68, "section": "9.6.2 Primary Endpoint", "exactText": "", "bboxes": [[71.9999999