INFO     rag_service.services.generation_service:generation_service.py:427 {"event": "rag_query_trace", "query": "Why was prior corticosteroid use chosen as a stratification factor?", "retrieval_query": null, "system_prompt_version": "440b2406", "retrieved_chunks": [{"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 92, "section": "Appendix 4 - Summary of Changes for Version 1.1", "score": 0.31174105, "scores": null, "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 36, "section": "4.2.2 Randomisation", "score": null, "scores": [0.24598956, 0.00047101185], "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 68, "section": "9.6.2 Primary Endpoint", "score": 0.103748634, "scores": null, "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 8, "section": "Study Design", "score": 0.0013939502, "scores": null, "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 23, "section": "1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects", "score": 0.0011513996, "scores": null, "had_context": true}], "context_char_count": 6970, "response": "Source: Protocol Ulcerative Colitis.pdf\n\nThe protocol does not explicitly state the rationale for why prior corticosteroid use was chosen as a stratification factor. However, the following contextual information is available:\n\n1. Prior corticosteroids treatment (yes/no) and consent to participate in PK substudy (yes/no) are the two randomisation stratification factors used in the trial, replacing the original stratification factors (elevated CRP levels and stable conventional treatment history) that were present in the earlier version of the protocol.\n[Section Appendix 4 - Summary of Changes for Version 1.1 \u00b7 p.92]\n\n2. The stratification by prior corticosteroids treatment is applied at the Randomisation Visit (Visit 2), with the information collected at Visit 1, to ensure balanced allocation across the three treatment arms (placebo, TJ301 300 mg, TJ301 600 mg).\n[Section 4.2.2 Randomisation \u00b7 p.36]\n\n3. The randomisation-stratification factors (prior corticosteroids treatment and consent to participate in PK substudy) are also used as fixed effects in the logistic regression model for the primary efficacy endpoint analysis, reflecting their role in controlling for potential confounding in the statistical analysis.\n[Section 9.6.2 Primary Endpoint \u00b7 p.68]\n\n4. From a clinical background perspective, corticosteroids are used for induction of remission in UC patients not responding to 5-ASA, and are associated with frequent and serious side effects, making prior exposure a clinically meaningful patient characteristic that could influence treatment response.\n[Section 1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects \u00b7 p.23]\n\nI don't have this information regarding the explicit stated justification for selecting prior corticosteroid use specifically as a stratification factor, as the protocol does not provide a direct rationale for this choice.", "sources": [{"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 92, "section": "Appendix 4 - Summary of Changes for Version 1.1", "exactText": "Randomisation will be stratified by prior corticosteroids treatment (yes/no) and consent to participate in PK substudy (yes/no).", "bboxes": [[52.59044647216797, 495.76949310302734, 771.7723999023438, 121.96722412109375]], "relevance": "high"}, {"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 36, "section": "4.2.2 Randomisation", "exactText": "Randomisation will be stratified by prior corticosteroids treatment (yes/no) and consent to participate in PK substudy (yes/no). This information will be collected at Visit 1 and will be used at Visit 2 for randomisation by stratification based on these factors.", "bboxes": [[72.0, 233.90398291015606, 538.44, 178.01497521784836], [72.12, 738.5039829101562, 555.8400000000001, 713.0949752178485]], "relevance": "high"}, {"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 68, "section": "9.6.2 Primary Endpoint", "exactText": "This binary outcome (remission status=yes/no) variable will be analysed by a logistic regression model with treatment and the randomisation-stratification factors (prior corticosteroids treatment and consent to participate in PK substudy) as fixed effects and the baseline full Mayo score as covariate.", "bboxes": [[71.99999999999997, 721.7039829101564, 558.8399999999996, 605.2149752178486]], "relevance": "medium"}, {"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 23, "section": "1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects", "exactText": "Oral, systemic corticosteroids are used for induction of remission in patients not responding to 5-ASA. Corticosteroids have frequent and occasionally serious side effects, e.g. hypothalamicpituitary-adrenal (HPA)-axis suppression, hyperglycaemia/insulin resistance, cataracts, and osteoporosis", "bboxes": [[71.99999999999972, 338.18398291015615, 552.8399999999998, 176.0949752178484]], "relevance": "low"}], "timing": {"response_cache_hit": false, "semantic_cache_hit": false, "chunks_compressed": true, "condense_ms": 0.000833999365568161, "query_condensed": false, "embedding_ms": 893.8863752409816, "embedding_cache_hit": false, "retrieval": {"chunk_cache_hit": false, "confidence_filter_applied": true, "confidence_filter_threshold": 0.04, "confidence_filter_bm25_threshold": null, "confidence_filter_input_count": 45, "confidence_filter_kept_count": 45, "confidence_filter_dropped_count": 0, "confidence_filter_kept_scores": [0.5737, 0.5121, 0.5115, 0.5086, 0.4961, 0.4806, 0.4635, 0.4581, 0.4551, 0.4499, 0.4451, 0.4404, 0.4328, 0.4259, 0.4225, 0.4217, 0.4138, 0.4128, 0.4125, 0.4119, 0.409, 0.4071, 0.4037, 0.4001, 0.3993, 0.3992, 0.3969, 0.3948, 0.3929, 0.3927, 0.3917, 0.3916, 0.3882, 0.3881, 0.3872, 0.3856, 0.3855, 0.3854, 0.3827, 0.3819, 0.3801, 0.3797, 0.3784, 0.3756, 0.3742], "confidence_filter_dropped_scores": [], "confidence_filter_bm25_only_scores_normalized": [], "hybrid_search": true, "cache_hit": true, "embedding_ms": 0.0, "db_search_ms": 5652.6285000145435, "hybri